1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,484
The developer who made the mistake is
relieved. They removed the file, added it to

2
00:00:09,524 --> 00:00:13,895
.gitignore, the list of paths Git
shouldn't track, committed and pushed.

3
00:00:13,935 --> 00:00:18,489
They search the code: nothing. The
repository on GitHub shows no .env file.

4
00:00:18,529 --> 00:00:22,533
The message in the team channel
says: "Removed, sorry about that."

5
00:00:22,873 --> 00:00:27,071
The textbook's judgment on that answer
is one sentence: deleting a file adds a

6
00:00:27,111 --> 00:00:28,538
commit and removes nothing.

7
00:00:28,878 --> 00:00:33,652
In this video you'll run the developer's
exact repair in the sandbox, see the search

8
00:00:33,692 --> 00:00:38,409
come back clean, and then print the key
from a commit four steps back, and from the

9
00:00:38,449 --> 00:00:42,879
server, through a release tag. And you'll
learn the only question that decides

10
00:00:42,919 --> 00:00:47,693
whether a leaked secret is dangerous, which
isn't a question about the repository at

11
00:00:47,733 --> 00:00:49,011
all. Hold that thought.

12
00:00:50,851 --> 00:00:54,437
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:54,477 --> 00:00:58,122
This video has two halves that
belong together in an incident.

14
00:00:58,462 --> 00:01:03,324
The first half is about the credential,
the token or key that proves to a service

15
00:01:03,364 --> 00:01:07,743
who is asking. If one leaks, what can
the holder reach, and for how long?

16
00:01:07,783 --> 00:01:12,524
That depends on its type, and the textbook
says most teams can't say which type

17
00:01:12,564 --> 00:01:13,856
their automation uses.

18
00:01:14,196 --> 00:01:18,948
The second half is about the repository:
why deletion, a forced push, and making the

19
00:01:18,988 --> 00:01:23,740
repository private don't remove a secret,
and how you measure where it is with three

20
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:24,766
built-in commands.

21
00:01:25,106 --> 00:01:29,960
The chapter insists on three layers, and
you should hold them apart for the rest of

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,144
this part. Git on your machine:
what history contains.

23
00:01:33,184 --> 00:01:36,504
GitHub: what is displayed,
scanned, blocked and retained.

24
00:01:36,544 --> 00:01:41,280
And the issuer of the credential, the
service that created it: whether the leaked

25
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:46,115
secret still works. The third layer is the
one tutorials leave out, and it's where

26
00:01:46,155 --> 00:01:50,949
incidents are decided. A secret is harmless
from the moment its issuer revokes it,

27
00:01:50,989 --> 00:01:55,666
meaning cancels it, and dangerous until
then, whatever you do to the repository.

28
00:01:56,006 --> 00:01:58,312
Every secret in the
transcripts is a dummy.

29
00:02:00,152 --> 00:02:01,730
Here's what you'll walk away with.

30
00:02:02,070 --> 00:02:03,482
After this video you can:

31
00:02:03,822 --> 00:02:07,016
rank credential types by what
a thief can reach with each;

32
00:02:07,356 --> 00:02:11,654
explain why a secret deleted in a later
commit is still in the repository;

33
00:02:11,994 --> 00:02:14,871
say where a force-pushed-away
commit can still be found;

34
00:02:15,211 --> 00:02:18,864
find a secret in every ref of a
repository with built-in commands;

35
00:02:19,204 --> 00:02:21,722
say which refs and tags
contain the secret.

36
00:02:23,562 --> 00:02:27,940
What a stolen credential can reach.
Seven rows, from narrow to wide.

37
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:33,122
The Actions GITHUB_TOKEN: the workflow's
repository, within the job's permissions.

38
00:02:33,162 --> 00:02:34,194
Lifetime: the job.

39
00:02:34,534 --> 00:02:39,464
A GitHub App installation token: the
installation's repositories and permissions.

40
00:02:39,504 --> 00:02:40,630
Lifetime: one hour.

41
00:02:40,970 --> 00:02:46,254
A GitHub App private key: it mints installation
tokens for every installation of the

42
00:02:46,294 --> 00:02:48,282
app. Lifetime: it never expires.

43
00:02:48,622 --> 00:02:53,901
A fine-grained personal access token: one
owner, optionally selected repositories,

44
00:02:53,941 --> 00:02:56,430
per-permission. Lifetime: configurable.

45
00:02:56,770 --> 00:03:00,501
A deploy key: one repository,
read or read-write. Long-lived.

46
00:03:00,841 --> 00:03:05,185
A personal SSH key: everything
the account can reach over SSH.

47
00:03:05,225 --> 00:03:05,963
Long-lived.

48
00:03:06,303 --> 00:03:11,326
A classic personal access token: every
repository and organization its owner can

49
00:03:11,366 --> 00:03:13,098
reach, by scope. Long-lived.

50
00:03:13,438 --> 00:03:16,560
Two automatic protections
exist, and both are narrow.

51
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:21,392
GitHub revokes its own tokens when they're
pushed to a public repository or gist.

52
00:03:21,432 --> 00:03:23,778
And it removes tokens unused for a year.

53
00:03:24,118 --> 00:03:29,397
Quick quiz. Which leak is worse? A, an
installation token that lives for one hour.

54
00:03:29,437 --> 00:03:33,548
B, the App private key that mints
such tokens and never expires.

55
00:03:33,588 --> 00:03:34,326
Your answer?

56
00:03:39,166 --> 00:03:43,619
B. The textbook calls the App private key
the row people forget, and gives a figure

57
00:03:43,659 --> 00:03:44,485
with its source.

58
00:03:44,825 --> 00:03:50,161
Of 4,802 leaked GitHub App private keys
tested in September 2026, 474 still

59
00:03:50,201 --> 00:03:56,111
authenticated, 44 of them with organization
admin access, according to GitGuardian.

60
00:03:56,151 --> 00:04:01,989
The conclusion: short-lived installation
tokens are only as safe as the long-lived

61
00:04:02,029 --> 00:04:03,423
key that mints them.

62
00:04:03,763 --> 00:04:08,810
A hierarchy for automation. The Phase 0
report derives an order from those facts.

63
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:12,139
It is marked as an inference,
not a GitHub statement.

64
00:04:12,179 --> 00:04:17,288
First: OIDC-issued cloud tokens and the
job-scoped GITHUB_TOKEN, wherever the work

65
00:04:17,328 --> 00:04:22,376
happens inside a workflow. Second: GitHub
App installation tokens, with the App's

66
00:04:22,416 --> 00:04:27,651
private key in a secrets manager. Third:
fine-grained personal access tokens with an

67
00:04:27,691 --> 00:04:32,801
expiry and organization approval. Fourth:
deploy keys, for read access to a single

68
00:04:32,841 --> 00:04:38,014
repository. Fifth and last: classic tokens
and shared machine users, with a written

69
00:04:38,054 --> 00:04:38,453
reason.

70
00:04:38,793 --> 00:04:42,889
Real compromises abuse tokens,
sessions and laptops, not Git.

71
00:04:42,929 --> 00:04:48,178
Three cases from the section. In April
2022, stolen OAuth tokens issued to two

72
00:04:48,218 --> 00:04:53,534
integrators were used to clone private
repositories of dozens of organizations.

73
00:04:53,574 --> 00:04:58,822
The lesson is that a third party's token
store is part of your attack surface.

74
00:04:59,162 --> 00:05:04,373
In December 2022, malware on an engineer's
laptop at a CI provider stole an already

75
00:05:04,413 --> 00:05:08,105
authenticated session, bypassing
two-factor authentication.

76
00:05:08,145 --> 00:05:13,166
The lesson is that two-factor authentication
protects the login, not the session

77
00:05:13,206 --> 00:05:14,178
that follows it.

78
00:05:14,518 --> 00:05:19,381
And in May 2026, a poisoned third-party
editor extension on an employee device led

79
00:05:19,421 --> 00:05:23,925
to exfiltration of GitHub-internal
repositories, and GitHub rotated critical

80
00:05:23,965 --> 00:05:28,888
secrets. For that third case the textbook
flags that attribution and details of the

81
00:05:28,928 --> 00:05:33,850
extension come from vendor reports, and
that one vendor gives the detection date as

82
00:05:33,890 --> 00:05:37,557
the nineteenth of May where GitHub
says the eighteenth of May.

83
00:05:37,897 --> 00:05:43,109
Token forensics. After a suspected compromise
the question is "what did this token

84
00:05:43,149 --> 00:05:48,234
do?". GitHub's audit log, an organization's
record of events, can be searched by

85
00:05:48,274 --> 00:05:53,486
token without storing the token itself:
you compute its SHA-256 and search for the

86
00:05:53,526 --> 00:05:53,806
hash.

87
00:05:54,146 --> 00:05:59,244
Two limits decide whether this works on
the day. A search by token hash returns no

88
00:05:59,284 --> 00:06:04,445
Git events: clones, fetches and pushes made
with the token have to be identified in

89
00:06:04,485 --> 00:06:09,646
an export of Git events data. And the
retention differs: the organization audit log

90
00:06:09,686 --> 00:06:14,909
covers 180 days, while Git events are an
Enterprise Cloud feature that the audit log

91
00:06:14,949 --> 00:06:19,984
retains for seven days. So to answer "was
the repository cloned with this token?"

92
00:06:20,024 --> 00:06:24,308
months later, streaming or export
must be set up before the incident.

93
00:06:24,648 --> 00:06:30,879
The scale, in three figures, each
with its source in section 21B.9.

94
00:06:30,919 --> 00:06:38,648
GitGuardian counted 28,649,024 new secrets
on public GitHub in 2025, up 34 percent.

95
00:06:38,688 --> 00:06:44,170
Of them, 1,275,105 were tied
to AI services, up 81 percent.

96
00:06:44,210 --> 00:06:51,752
64 percent of the secrets confirmed valid
in 2022 were still valid when retested.

97
00:06:52,092 --> 00:06:56,734
And internal repositories were about six
times more likely than public ones to

98
00:06:56,774 --> 00:07:01,595
contain a hardcoded secret. The textbook
reads the last two for you: "still valid

99
00:07:01,635 --> 00:07:05,797
years later" means that detection
without revocation achieves nothing.

100
00:07:05,837 --> 00:07:10,718
"Internal repositories are worse" means
that privacy is being used as a substitute

101
00:07:10,758 --> 00:07:11,438
for hygiene.

102
00:07:11,778 --> 00:07:17,321
Why deletion is not removal. In one sentence:
a commit is a permanent snapshot, so a

103
00:07:17,361 --> 00:07:22,904
later commit that deletes a file leaves
every earlier snapshot intact, and moving or

104
00:07:22,944 --> 00:07:28,221
hiding refs changes who can easily find
the old snapshot, not whether it exists.

105
00:07:28,261 --> 00:07:32,740
A ref is a name, such as a branch
or a tag, that points at a commit.

106
00:07:33,080 --> 00:07:35,882
Three operations are commonly
mistaken for removal.

107
00:07:36,222 --> 00:07:41,036
Commit a deletion. What it changes: it adds
a new commit whose tree lacks the file.

108
00:07:41,076 --> 00:07:45,714
Where the secret still is: in every commit
between the one that added it and the

109
00:07:45,754 --> 00:07:49,691
deletion, reachable from the branch,
from tags, from other branches.

110
00:07:50,031 --> 00:07:54,904
Amend or reset, then force-push, which makes
the server's branch point at different

111
00:07:54,944 --> 00:07:58,573
commits. What it changes: it
moves a ref to different commits.

112
00:07:58,613 --> 00:08:03,308
Where the secret still is: in the old
commits, which stay in the server's object

113
00:08:03,348 --> 00:08:08,162
database, in every clone and fork that
fetched them, and on GitHub in cached views

114
00:08:08,202 --> 00:08:10,884
and through pull requests
that reference them.

115
00:08:11,224 --> 00:08:16,369
Make the repository private, or delete it.
What it changes: who may read through the

116
00:08:16,409 --> 00:08:21,430
normal interface. Where the secret still
is: in every clone and fork made while it

117
00:08:21,470 --> 00:08:26,553
was public, and in the fork network, a
repository and all its forks, whose Git data

118
00:08:26,593 --> 00:08:27,725
is stored together.

119
00:08:28,065 --> 00:08:33,487
GitHub states the second and the third row
itself, and researchers have shown both

120
00:08:33,527 --> 00:08:39,082
to be exploitable at scale. In 2024, 40
valid API keys in commits from deleted forks

121
00:08:39,122 --> 00:08:44,677
of three commonly forked repositories. And
in 2025, commits orphaned by force pushes

122
00:08:44,717 --> 00:08:49,806
enumerated from public event archives
and scanned, which yielded thousands of

123
00:08:49,846 --> 00:08:50,339
secrets.

124
00:08:50,679 --> 00:08:55,568
Finding it. You need three commands, and
you need to know what each one can't see.

125
00:08:55,908 --> 00:09:00,434
git log -S with a string, the pickaxe,
lists commits in which the number of

126
00:09:00,474 --> 00:09:05,366
occurrences of the string changed: the
commits that added it and the commits that

127
00:09:05,406 --> 00:09:08,959
removed it. Without --all it
walks only the current branch.

128
00:09:09,299 --> 00:09:14,274
git log -G with a regular expression
lists commits whose diff has an added or

129
00:09:14,314 --> 00:09:19,551
removed line that matches. Use it when you
know the shape of a secret and not its

130
00:09:19,591 --> 00:09:22,221
value, and with --all to cover every ref.

131
00:09:22,561 --> 00:09:27,019
git grep with a pattern and the list of
all commits searches the content of every

132
00:09:27,059 --> 00:09:31,127
commit's tree, not the diffs. It answers
"in which snapshots is the secret

133
00:09:31,167 --> 00:09:35,403
present?", which is the exposure. The
pickaxe answers "where did it enter and

134
00:09:35,443 --> 00:09:35,847
leave?".

135
00:09:36,187 --> 00:09:42,955
And to turn a commit into scope: git branch
-a --contains and git tag --contains.

136
00:09:43,295 --> 00:09:45,867
All of these are 🟢 SAFE:
they change nothing.

137
00:09:47,707 --> 00:09:52,665
Analogy, from the textbook. Publishing a
correction in tomorrow's newspaper doesn't

138
00:09:52,705 --> 00:09:56,098
recall yesterday's edition
from the people who bought it.

139
00:09:56,138 --> 00:10:00,856
Withdrawing yesterday's edition from your
own archive, which is the force push,

140
00:10:00,896 --> 00:10:02,362
doesn't recall it either.

141
00:10:02,702 --> 00:10:07,379
The analogy breaks for unpushed commits: an
edition that never left the building can

142
00:10:07,419 --> 00:10:09,232
be pulped. That's the next video.

143
00:10:09,572 --> 00:10:14,086
So the dividing line is the first push.
Before it, the secret exists in one

144
00:10:14,126 --> 00:10:18,032
repository, yours, and you can
rewrite your own unpushed commits.

145
00:10:18,072 --> 00:10:23,072
After it, the secret exists in every
repository that fetched those commits, and you

146
00:10:23,112 --> 00:10:24,347
control none of them.

147
00:10:24,687 --> 00:10:29,036
Now put the two halves of this video
together. After the first push you can't

148
00:10:29,076 --> 00:10:32,797
un-publish. What you can do is make
the published thing worthless.

149
00:10:32,837 --> 00:10:37,528
That happens at the issuer, by revocation,
and it works against every copy at once:

150
00:10:37,568 --> 00:10:42,316
the one on the server, the one in a fork,
the one in a colleague's clone, the one in

151
00:10:42,356 --> 00:10:46,875
a log. Nothing you do in Git has that
property. So there's the question from the

152
00:10:46,915 --> 00:10:50,523
opening: not "is the file gone?",
but "does the key still work?"

153
00:10:50,863 --> 00:10:55,055
And how urgent it is depends on the
first half: the type of credential.

154
00:10:55,095 --> 00:10:58,869
A job token that died with its
job an hour ago is one situation.

155
00:10:58,909 --> 00:11:01,790
An App private key that
never expires is another.

156
00:11:03,630 --> 00:11:08,362
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Write "leaked commit" in the middle of the

157
00:11:08,402 --> 00:11:12,657
page. Around it, write every place
where a copy of that commit may live.

158
00:11:12,697 --> 00:11:15,103
How many can you name? Say them out loud.

159
00:11:19,943 --> 00:11:24,933
Compare with the picture. A server branch,
a server tag, an old pull request ref and

160
00:11:24,973 --> 00:11:28,945
cached views, a fork or the fork
network, and clones with a CI log.

161
00:11:28,985 --> 00:11:33,855
A deletion commit, a forced push and going
private each change one thing, and none

162
00:11:33,895 --> 00:11:38,885
of them reaches every copy. Revoking at the
issuer does: every copy becomes a string

163
00:11:38,925 --> 00:11:40,022
that opens nothing.

164
00:11:40,362 --> 00:11:44,988
Read the root-cause line: the secret was
pushed, so it exists in every repository

165
00:11:45,028 --> 00:11:46,543
that fetched those commits.

166
00:11:48,383 --> 00:11:49,839
Step 1: the leak.

167
00:11:50,179 --> 00:11:56,244
Into the lab. Eight commits and two tags.
Four commits back, in 0805fd8, "Add

168
00:11:56,284 --> 00:12:01,555
staging settings", a git add -A
swept a local .env into the commit.

169
00:12:01,595 --> 00:12:07,104
The file holds a dummy key. Notice
the tag v0.2.0 one commit above it.

170
00:12:07,444 --> 00:12:09,792
Step 2: the usual first reaction.

171
00:12:10,132 --> 00:12:15,423
git rm --cached is 🟡 CAUTION: it removes
the file from the index, and the working

172
00:12:15,463 --> 00:12:20,886
tree copy stays. git commit is 🟢 SAFE. After
this commit: what does git grep on the

173
00:12:20,926 --> 00:12:25,558
tip say, and what does git show of
the same path four commits back say?

174
00:12:25,598 --> 00:12:26,611
Say it out loud.

175
00:12:31,451 --> 00:12:36,456
The file list has no .env. git grep
finds nothing and exits with status 1.

176
00:12:36,496 --> 00:12:40,137
That's what the developer
saw, and why they felt safe.

177
00:12:40,177 --> 00:12:44,091
And git show HEAD~4:.env prints
the key. The tip is clean.

178
00:12:44,131 --> 00:12:47,091
The commit that added
the file still has it.

179
00:12:47,431 --> 00:12:53,921
Here's main as a picture, before the repair.
Eight commits, with 0805fd8 four from

180
00:12:53,961 --> 00:12:57,346
the top, and the tag
v0.2.0 right above it.

181
00:12:57,686 --> 00:13:03,503
The repair is one new commit on top,
d42d1a1. Nothing below it changed.

182
00:13:03,543 --> 00:13:09,937
0805fd8 still holds the file, and v0.2.0
still points into the affected range.

183
00:13:10,277 --> 00:13:12,751
Step 3: where did it enter and leave?

184
00:13:13,091 --> 00:13:16,757
The pickaxe lists commits where
the number of occurrences changed.

185
00:13:16,797 --> 00:13:19,565
How many commits, and which?
Make your prediction.

186
00:13:24,405 --> 00:13:28,858
Two: 0805fd8, which introduced the
key, and d42d1a1, which deleted it.

187
00:13:28,898 --> 00:13:33,864
Everything between them contains it and
isn't listed, because the count didn't

188
00:13:33,904 --> 00:13:39,191
change there. Remember that when you read
pickaxe output: it shows the edges of the

189
00:13:39,231 --> 00:13:40,924
exposure, not the exposure.

190
00:13:41,264 --> 00:13:46,727
With -p you see the lines, and with
--diff-filter=A only commits that added a file,

191
00:13:46,767 --> 00:13:52,163
which is the introduction. The textbook's
remark: that one transcript answers four

192
00:13:52,203 --> 00:13:56,273
assessment questions. Which
commit, who, when, and which file.

193
00:13:56,613 --> 00:13:58,724
Step 4: the shape, not the value.

194
00:13:59,064 --> 00:14:04,212
A regular expression, a pattern that describes
text by its shape, for the family of

195
00:14:04,252 --> 00:14:08,275
dummy secrets, over all refs. Do
you expect the same two commits?

196
00:14:08,315 --> 00:14:09,275
Say it out loud.

197
00:14:14,115 --> 00:14:17,895
Three. The third, 54093fe, is
"Add rerank debugging notebook".

198
00:14:17,935 --> 00:14:22,762
The second command shows where: a bearer
token captured in the output cell of a

199
00:14:22,802 --> 00:14:26,151
notebook, on a branch that
was pushed and never merged.

200
00:14:26,191 --> 00:14:31,203
Nobody had reported it. Notebook outputs
leak secrets that the author never typed.

201
00:14:31,543 --> 00:14:34,340
Step 5: in which snapshots is it present?

202
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:39,846
Five snapshots contain the key. The first
column is the commit, shortened by cut.

203
00:14:39,886 --> 00:14:44,410
Four are on main, and one, starting
7fae871, is on a teammate's branch.

204
00:14:44,450 --> 00:14:48,267
Then the summary: .env in five
commits, the notebook in one.

205
00:14:48,307 --> 00:14:52,959
On a large repository the argument
list becomes too long for one command.

206
00:14:52,999 --> 00:14:55,851
The textbook gives the
piped form with xargs.

207
00:14:56,191 --> 00:14:57,812
Step 6: which refs?

208
00:14:58,152 --> 00:15:04,006
The local main, the remote-tracking main,
a remote-tracking feature branch, and the

209
00:15:04,046 --> 00:15:04,787
tag v0.2.0.

210
00:15:05,127 --> 00:15:09,080
That list is the scope on the Git side:
every ref from which the commit is

211
00:15:09,120 --> 00:15:09,620
reachable.

212
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:11,569
Step 7: the server.

213
00:15:11,909 --> 00:15:16,825
git push is 🟡 CAUTION. After the deletion
commit is pushed: what does the server say

214
00:15:16,865 --> 00:15:20,837
about the tip of main, and what
about the tag? Make your prediction.

215
00:15:25,677 --> 00:15:31,658
The tip of main: no match, exit status 1.
The tag v0.2.0: line 1 of .env, with the

216
00:15:31,698 --> 00:15:37,605
key. The server agrees on both counts. The
tag points into the affected range and

217
00:15:37,645 --> 00:15:40,616
serves the file to anyone who fetches it.

218
00:15:40,956 --> 00:15:45,893
Even a search over all refs and reflogs
doesn't see unreachable objects without

219
00:15:45,933 --> 00:15:48,476
reflog entries, or other people's clones.

220
00:15:50,316 --> 00:15:51,964
Five mistakes to watch for.

221
00:15:52,304 --> 00:15:57,346
Saying "I removed it" after a deletion
commit. Root cause: git rm changes the next

222
00:15:57,386 --> 00:16:02,553
snapshot; every earlier snapshot is an
immutable object and still contains the file.

223
00:16:02,893 --> 00:16:07,597
Checking only the tip, or only the current
branch. Root cause: git grep without

224
00:16:07,637 --> 00:16:12,402
commits searches the working tree's tracked
files, and the pickaxe without --all

225
00:16:12,442 --> 00:16:16,305
walks one branch; tags and other
branches still reach the commit.

226
00:16:16,645 --> 00:16:21,589
Force-pushing and considering the commit
gone. Root cause: a forced push moves a

227
00:16:21,629 --> 00:16:26,698
ref; the old commits stay in the server's
object database, in clones and forks, in

228
00:16:26,738 --> 00:16:29,066
cached views and behind pull requests.

229
00:16:29,406 --> 00:16:34,362
Making the repository private as a response.
Root cause: that changes who may read

230
00:16:34,402 --> 00:16:39,115
from now on, not what was fetched while
it was public or what the fork network

231
00:16:39,155 --> 00:16:39,481
holds.

232
00:16:39,821 --> 00:16:44,573
Adding the file to .gitignore and stopping
there. Root cause: ignoring a tracked

233
00:16:44,613 --> 00:16:49,425
file changes nothing; and neither step
touches whether the key still works at its

234
00:16:49,465 --> 00:16:49,845
issuer.

235
00:16:51,685 --> 00:16:56,955
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's
description of how these leaks happen in your

236
00:16:56,995 --> 00:17:02,462
field: an LLM key is created in a browser
tab, pasted into a notebook or a .env, and

237
00:17:02,502 --> 00:17:07,248
committed by git add -A or by an agent
that stages everything it touched.

238
00:17:07,589 --> 00:17:12,416
Take that as an incident on an AI team.
Monday, an engineer commits a .env with a

239
00:17:12,456 --> 00:17:17,223
provider key. Wednesday, they notice,
delete the file in a new commit, and push.

240
00:17:17,263 --> 00:17:21,910
Friday, a scanner run by someone outside
the company reports the key as valid.

241
00:17:22,250 --> 00:17:26,321
What the team should have asked on
Wednesday isn't "is the file gone?"

242
00:17:26,361 --> 00:17:30,021
but two other things. First, at
the issuer: is the key revoked?

243
00:17:30,061 --> 00:17:34,837
It wasn't. Nobody thought of the provider's
console, because the repository looked

244
00:17:34,877 --> 00:17:39,770
clean. Second, in Git: which commits, which
refs? With the three commands the answer

245
00:17:39,810 --> 00:17:44,527
takes two minutes, and in a case like the
demonstration it includes a release tag

246
00:17:44,567 --> 00:17:45,996
and a colleague's branch.

247
00:17:46,336 --> 00:17:53,547
And one more question, from the first half:
what type of credential was it, and what

248
00:17:53,587 --> 00:18:00,626
can it reach? A provider key with no expiry
and full account scope is the wide end

249
00:18:00,666 --> 00:18:01,748
of the table.

