267
00:19:18,994 --> 00:19:20,654
You should now be able to say:

268
00:19:20,994 --> 00:19:26,033
A credential's danger is its reach and its
lifetime: from a job token that dies with

269
00:19:26,073 --> 00:19:30,266
the job to a classic token or an App
private key that does not expire.

270
00:19:30,606 --> 00:19:35,710
A deletion commit adds a snapshot without
the file; every earlier snapshot still has

271
00:19:35,750 --> 00:19:35,894
it.

272
00:19:36,234 --> 00:19:41,003
A forced push moves a ref, and going
private changes who may read from now on;

273
00:19:41,043 --> 00:19:45,998
neither removes the commit from clones,
forks, cached views or pull request refs.

274
00:19:46,338 --> 00:19:51,587
The pickaxe shows where a secret entered
and left, git grep over all commits shows

275
00:19:51,627 --> 00:19:56,683
every snapshot that contains it, and
--contains turns the commit into a list of

276
00:19:56,723 --> 00:19:57,005
refs.

277
00:19:57,345 --> 00:20:02,324
The exposure window starts at the first
push, and the only step that works against

278
00:20:02,364 --> 00:20:04,710
every copy is revocation at the issuer.

